From 207503305b11f74f2653d7c8be549cea3ba5be73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <florian.stosse@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:29:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [kernel] print _Alignas with the base type, never inside the
 declarator

_Alignas is an alignment specifier, part of the declaration specifiers
(C17 6.7.5). The printer emitted it in three invalid places:

- after the declarator of a struct or union member:
    uint64_t u64[2] _Alignas(16) ;
- after the '*' of a pointer declarator (globals, locals and members):
    int *_Alignas(16) gp;
    int (*_Alignas(16) gfp)(int );
- after the '*' of a pointer element of an array:
    char * _Alignas(8)gap[2];

GCC, Clang and Frama-C itself reject all of them, so the output of
-print or -ocode could not be compiled or re-parsed (E-ACSL's generated
code included).

Cil_printer.typ now passes the alignment down through TPtr, TArray and
TFun to the base type, where it is printed after the type name and its
qualifiers (int _Alignas(16) *gp;), and fieldinfo uses the same path as
vdecl instead of appending the specifier after the member name.

Declarations whose alignment was already valid only change spacing
(char  _Alignas(16)la[4] becomes char _Alignas(16) la[4]), which updates
the oracles of tests/syntax/alignas_typing_constrains and
plugins/rte/tests/basic/unaligned_pointer. The new test
tests/pretty_printing/alignas.c re-parses the printed code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
